Preferences Implementation for StretchRes
=========================================

This directory contains the preference bundle implementation for the StretchRes tweak.

Preference Bundle Structure
--------------------------

The preference bundle is built using Theos's bundle.makefile integration and 
consists of the following components:

1. Root.plist (project root) - The main preference specifier file that defines
   all UI elements including:
   - Group headers and section titles
   - PSLabel elements for displaying current/original resolution
   - PSNumberTextField for custom width/height input
   - PSButton for Apply and Reset actions
   - PSRadioButton for preset selection (Native, 16:9, Custom, Portrait, Landscape)
   - PSToggleSwitch for Enable/Disable and Auto Apply options
   - PSFooter credits and compatibility info

2. Tweak.x - The main tweak implementation that handles:
   - Resolution changing via IOMobileGraphicsFamily plist manipulation
   - User preference storage using NSUserDefaults
   - XPC notification to SpringBoard for display reload
   - Device detection and resolution validation
   - Respring configuration restoration

3. Info.plist - Tweak metadata including iOS 15.0 minimum deployment target

Preference Storage Mechanism
---------------------------

User preferences are stored using NSUserDefaults with the following keys:
- last_preset: Selected preset type (native, 16:9, custom, portrait, landscape)
- custom_width: Custom width value in pixels
- custom_height: Custom height value in pixels
- tweak_enabled: Enable/disable toggle state
- auto_apply_after_respring: Auto-apply configuration after respring

The preference bundle filename is "StretchRes" as specified in Root.plist:
<prefs:filename>StretchRes</prefs:filename>

UI Design Philosophy
-------------------

- All UI elements are defined in Root.plist using PSPreferences specifier types
- No storyboard or XIB files are used, keeping the tweak lightweight and
  compatible with iOS 15 without requiring Xcode or Interface Builder
- The interface is designed to be compatible with iPhone 6s/7 series native 
  resolutions and smaller older device screens
- Device-compatible presets auto-detect the device model and suggest appropriate
  resolution values based on native bounds

